import{_ as t,o as a,c as s,ag as i}from"./chunks/framework.D1z9wg1y.js";const f=JSON.parse('{"title":"Specs and gates","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"agentic/specs-and-gates.md","filePath":"agentic/specs-and-gates.md"}'),o={name:"agentic/specs-and-gates.md"};function n(h,e,d,l,r,c){return a(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="specs-and-gates" tabindex="-1">Specs and gates <a class="header-anchor" href="#specs-and-gates" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Specs and gates&quot;">​</a></h1><p>An agent that has no fixed goal stops when the work looks done. A critic that has no fixed benchmark passes what looks right. So each part of a plan has a <em>spec</em>, written before the build, and each step of the loop ends at a <em>gate</em>: a command whose exit status says pass or fail. This page describes what a spec holds, how its facts are checked, and the gates that a part passes.</p><h2 id="what-a-spec-holds" tabindex="-1">What a spec holds <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-a-spec-holds" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What a spec holds&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The spec of a part is what the builder builds to and what the critic judges against. It has six blocks:</p><ol><li><strong>Done when.</strong> The acceptance criteria, as clauses. <strong>Every clause is checkable by a command</strong>: a test that fails before the change, a <code>grep</code> that finds nothing, a number with its tolerance and the reason for it. &quot;Works&quot;, &quot;is clean&quot; and &quot;matches the old code&quot; give a critic nothing to fail. A clause with <em>every</em> or <em>each</em> names its finite set, because the critic blocks only on a failure in that set.</li><li><strong>Decided at the edges.</strong> What the code does at each edge that the part reaches: for a numerical method <code>NaN</code> and <code>Inf</code>, a zero or subnormal input, an empty or a one-element case, the other precision. The list of edges for each kind of part is the <a href="./edges">edge catalogue</a>. An edge left open is found by a critic, one per round, as a blocking defect, so the planner decides each one in advance, or the part waits for you.</li><li><strong>Tests catch.</strong> For each clause that a test covers, the <a href="./mutants">mutant</a> that the test must catch: &quot;deleting the lower bound of the step factor makes the controller test fail&quot;. Without a named mutant, a test that passes with the feature deleted passes the loop.</li><li><strong>Files.</strong> The files that the part creates or changes, all in one repository. This is the part&#39;s row of the <a href="./tasks#the-collision-map">collision map</a>.</li><li><strong>Not in this part.</strong> What a builder might add and must not, with the part that owns it.</li><li><strong>Traps.</strong> A failure that an earlier attempt met, with its cause.</li></ol><p>A spec does not prescribe the order of the builder&#39;s work. The steps of a build are in the builder&#39;s own instructions, and a longer brief improves the first build more than the result.</p><h2 id="the-spec-delta-format" tabindex="-1">The spec-delta format <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-spec-delta-format" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The spec-delta format&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A new plan writes the spec of a <code>build</code> or <code>reviewed</code> part as a file of its own, in the spec-delta format. It states the base as requirements and the change as a delta, and each requirement has scenarios with commands:</p><div class="language-markdown vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">markdown</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-light-font-weight:bold;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;--shiki-dark-font-weight:bold;">### Requirement: The step factor stays in its bounds</span></span>\n<span class="line"></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">The controller SHALL return a factor in [0.2, 5.0] for every finite error estimate.</span></span>\n<span class="line"></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-light-font-weight:bold;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;--shiki-dark-font-weight:bold;">#### Scenario: A zero error estimate</span></span>\n<span class="line"></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#E36209;--shiki-dark:#FFAB70;">-</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> WHEN </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">`step_factor(0.0)`</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> is called</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#E36209;--shiki-dark:#FFAB70;">-</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> THEN it returns </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">`5.0`</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#E36209;--shiki-dark:#FFAB70;">-</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> check: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">`julia --project -e &#39;using Pendulum; Pendulum.step_factor(0.0) == 5.0 || exit(1)&#39;`</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#E36209;--shiki-dark:#FFAB70;">-</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> catches: MU1, the upper bound removed: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">`test/controller.jl`</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, testset &quot;bounds&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">section</th><th style="text-align:left;">holds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>1 · Living spec</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">each fact of the base that the change relies on, as a requirement whose <code>check:</code> passes on the base</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>2 · Change proposal</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">why, what is in and out of scope, the files, the commits, the seams</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>3 · Delta</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the requirements that the part modifies, adds and removes, each with its scenarios</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>4 · What stays from the plan</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the named mutants, the decisions that bind the part, the traps</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Each decided edge is a scenario. A named mutant is killed by a kept test, never by a command that runs once. A block stands on its own: a scenario that says &quot;as above&quot; fails the validator.</p><h2 id="the-premise-check" tabindex="-1">The premise check <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-premise-check" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The premise check&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A plan rests on facts: what a function returns, what a tool prints, which line holds a call. A fact that is false sends the builder in the wrong direction, and the loop finds it only after a round. Two checks run before the build:</p><ul><li><strong>A <code>reader</code> agent measures the facts of each spec.</strong> It lists each fact that the spec states, such as a count, a <code>file:line</code>, a version or what a tool does, and marks each one <code>holds</code>, <code>false</code> or <code>cannot</code>, with the command or the file that it read. The planner fixes every <code>false</code> fact. A reader in its own context does the check, because a planner reads its own facts as true.</li><li><strong><code>spec-validate.py</code> runs every premise on the base.</strong> It checks the structure of the spec and runs each <code>check:</code> of the living spec on the base commit. Planning ends when it passes. A check that needs a GPU runs from a Kaimon session, because the shell sandbox has no GPU; without that run, the verdict is <code>UNMEASURED</code>, never a pass.</li></ul><p>The check finds false facts, not stale ones: a premise that was true when the spec was written can go stale when another part merges. A <code>file:line</code> in a spec therefore names the commit that it was read at.</p><h2 id="the-gates" tabindex="-1">The gates <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-gates" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The gates&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A gate is a command with an exit status. The loop moves on only when the gate passes.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">gate</th><th style="text-align:left;">when</th><th style="text-align:left;">what it checks</th><th style="text-align:left;">exit status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><a href="./../components/scripts#spec-gate-py"><code>spec-gate.py</code></a></td><td style="text-align:left;">before a <code>build</code> part starts</td><td style="text-align:left;">the spec holds <em>Decided at the edges</em> and <em>Tests catch</em>; its files lie in one repository; a spec-delta spec passes <code>spec-validate.py</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>PASS</code> or <code>EXEMPT</code> 0; <code>MISSING</code>, <code>INVALID</code> or <code>SPANS</code> 1; <code>UNMEASURED</code> 2</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><a href="./../components/scripts#spec-validate-py"><code>spec-validate.py --head</code></a></td><td style="text-align:left;">each critic round of a spec-delta part</td><td style="text-align:left;">every <code>check:</code> of the delta on the branch head</td><td style="text-align:left;">0 when every check passes</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><a href="./../components/scripts#gate-jl-and-gate-test-jl"><code>gate.jl</code></a></td><td style="text-align:left;">by hand, for a Julia part: no skill or agent calls it</td><td style="text-align:left;">the affected tests, the named mutants, the generated mutants, the <a href="./static-checks#what-a-change-adds-to-the-method-table">dispatch delta</a>, the format</td><td style="text-align:left;">0 pass, 1 fail, 2 bad arguments, 3 no verdict</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><a href="./../harness-command#test"><code>harness test</code></a></td><td style="text-align:left;">every round of a part in the harness itself</td><td style="text-align:left;">the whole Python suite, the hook probe and the verb sweep</td><td style="text-align:left;">0 when every case passes</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">the test suite</td><td style="text-align:left;">beside each critic round</td><td style="text-align:left;">the full suite of the repository, run by <code>julia-test-runner</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the suite&#39;s own</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">CI</td><td style="text-align:left;">for a spec-delta part, on each pushed head</td><td style="text-align:left;">every job of the repository&#39;s workflows</td><td style="text-align:left;">the job conclusions</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A round passes only when the critic says PASS and the suite is green, and for a spec-delta part also every check and every CI job of the head. A failed check goes to the fix builder as a blocking defect, verbatim, beside the critic&#39;s defects.</p><h3 id="the-steps-of-gate-jl" tabindex="-1">The steps of <code>gate.jl</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-steps-of-gate-jl" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The steps of `gate.jl`&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>gate.jl</code> runs five steps on a Julia package against a base, by default <code>origin/main</code>, and writes its report to <code>gate.md</code>:</p><ol><li><strong><code>tests</code></strong>: the test files that the diff reaches, with <code>run-tests.jl affected</code>, and the quality tests.</li><li><strong><code>named</code></strong>: the named mutants, with <code>mutate.jl --warm</code>. A mutant that survives fails the step.</li><li><strong><code>generated</code></strong>: a sweep of operator mutants over the diff. It reports, and never fails.</li><li><strong><code>dispatch</code></strong>: <code>dispatch-delta.jl</code> against the base: new ambiguities, piracies, abstract fields and stale explicit imports fail.</li><li><strong><code>format</code></strong>: JuliaFormatter on the changed files.</li></ol><p>A step that reports gives INFO, which counts as a pass. A step that gives no verdict, such as a base that does not load, makes the gate exit 3 when no step fails: no verdict is never a pass.</p><h2 id="why-the-gates-are-commands" tabindex="-1">Why the gates are commands <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-the-gates-are-commands" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why the gates are commands&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An instruction such as &quot;check for method ambiguities&quot; works only when the agent reads it and acts on it. A gate works every time, and its result does not depend on the model. Where a rule of the harness can be a command, it becomes one, and its prose goes. <a href="./static-checks">Static checks</a> lists the checks that replace prose rules, and those that are planned.</p>',25)])])}const g=t(o,[["render",n]]);export{f as __pageData,g as default};
